> Applied Combinatorics.  First, for what I know, they use simplified
> Mandarin (much less kanji), second, they can build up kanji gradually
> (most complex kanji are combinations of some simple basic forms), third,
> they employ a lot of Meta characters (similar to our shift key), fourth,
> they need and get special training.

Not so difficult these days!

Most Asian nations have a phonetic alphabet by which they type the
"sound" of the characters.  Mainland China uses roman characters in a
system it calls "pin yin" (spell-sound).

Because many characters have the same sounds, computers enable this in
a way typewriters couldn't. By way of example, if I type "ni", my PC
screen shows a small auto-complete menu with all the characters
pronounced "ni", the most common ones first.  If I ignore the menu and
type "hao", it picks the two characters most commonly used in
combination and pronounced "ni hao" (=E4=BD=A0=E5=A5=BD=3Dhello).  Microsof=
t's input
system is so smart I almost never have to manually select a character
from the list.  I press alt-shift to switch languages.

I would assume the Japanese use their hiragana alphabet to do the same
sort of thing.

> > 2) Do you have lower-case and upper-case characters?
